{"hits": {"hits": [{"created": "1997-08-05T00:00:00+00:00", "updated": "2025-02-14T18:21:00.136883+00:00", "links": {"bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=latex-eu", "latex-us": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=latex-us", "json": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=json", "json-expanded": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=json-expanded", "cv": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/83667?format=cv", "citations": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A83667"}, "id": "83667", "metadata": {"authors": [{"full_name": "Coleman, Sidney R."}, {"full_name": "Gross, David J."}], "publication_info": [{"journal_volume": "31", "page_end": "854", "year": 1973, "journal_record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214495"}, "page_start": "851", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.Lett."}], "texkeys": ["Coleman:1973sx"], "imprints": [{"date": "1973-09-24"}], "abstracts": [{"value": "A renormalizable field theory is said to be asymptotically free if the origin of coupling-constant space is an ultraviolet-stable fixed point in the sense of Wilson. Asymptotically free theories are of great interest because they have almost-canonical light-cone singularities, and thus predict phenomena very close to Bjorken scaling. All known examples of asymptotically free theories involve non-Abelian gauge fields. We show that this is not coincidence: No renormalizable field theory without non-Abelian gauge fields can be asymptotically free."}], "control_number": 83667, "dois": [{"value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.31.851"}], "titles": [{"title": "Price of asymptotic freedom"}], "author_count": 2, "document_type": ["article"]}}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?sort=mostcited&q=j%20Phys.Rev.Lett.%2C31%2C851&size=3&page=1&fields=control_number,titles.title,authors.full_name,author_count,publication